  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>The strength of acidic character of boron trihalides depends upon <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p&#960;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>p&#960;</mi></math> back bonding.</p><p>In boron trihalides, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p&#960;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>p&#960;</mi></math> -back bonding occurs due to empty orbital of boron and filled orbitals of halogens.</p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0006_48914071c2b3.png\" style=\"width: 398px; height: 255px;\" /></p><p>The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>p&#960;</mi><mo>-</mo><mi>p&#960;</mi></math> back bonding is shown maximum by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>, as the size of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></math> are small and comparatively same. Due to this effect tendency of accepting lone-pair of electrons of boron decreases as size of halogen increases. The order of size of halogens are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Cl</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi>Br</mi><mo>&#60;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math>. Thus, acidic nature is in order</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>BF</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>BCl</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>BBr</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#60;</mo><msub><mi>BI</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math></p>"
  }
